# Node
/service/internal/ui/node_modules/
/service/internal/ui/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# A LOCAL `npm run build:prod` writes a real bundle here. It must not be
# committed while the Dockerfile still has a Node stage: the deployed UI
# comes from that stage, so a committed bundle would be a second source
# of truth that nothing rebuilds and nobody serves. Only the placeholder
# dist/index.html is tracked (see the long note above). Building locally
# is still the way to typecheck the SPA before pushing — the Docker build
# is where a TS error would otherwise surface, eight minutes in.
/service/internal/ui/dist/assets/

# NOTE: internal/ui/dist/ is deliberately NOT ignored. The Go binary
# embeds it with go:embed, and an embed of a missing directory is a
# COMPILE error, not a warning — so `go build`, `go test` and CI all
# need something there even though nobody looks at it. The template
# this repo was copied from ignored the directory, which fails with a
# confusing "pattern dist: no matching files found".
#
# Unlike sc-component-observatory, which commits a real built bundle
# and has no Node stage at all, the committed file here is a PLACEHOLDER
# index.html: service/Dockerfile still builds the SPA in a Node stage
# and overwrites dist/ with the result. So the deployed UI comes from
# the Dockerfile, and the committed file exists only to let the Go
# toolchain compile outside Docker.
#
# Collapse this to observatory's arrangement (commit the real bundle,
# drop the Node stage) when the SPA is worth building — i.e. at slice 3,
# when it grows past the one Tenants page. Until then, do not read the
# committed dist as what production serves.

